Manage Secure User Access in Microsoft 365

Date: September 3, 2026
Platform / Environment: Microsoft Learn — Microsoft 365 / Microsoft Entra ID

What I Did

Completed the Microsoft Learn module Manage secure user access in Microsoft 365.

The module covered several tools and controls used to secure user authentication and access, including:

* Microsoft Entra Conditional Access
* Multi-factor authentication (MFA)
* Passwordless authentication
* Self-service password management
* Microsoft Entra Smart Lockout
* Security Defaults
* Pass-through authentication
* Sign-in logs for investigating authentication issues

What I Learned

I learned how different identity and access controls work together to protect Microsoft 365 users.

A key area was Conditional Access, which can be used to apply access requirements based on factors such as the user, device, application, location, or risk.

I also learned how MFA, passwordless authentication and Security Defaults can strengthen authentication, while tools such as Smart Lockout help protect against repeated authentication attempts.

The module also introduced using sign-in logs to investigate authentication problems and understand why a user’s sign-in may have succeeded or failed.

Key Takeaway

Secure access is not just about having a strong password.

Microsoft 365 provides multiple layers of identity protection, and understanding how they work together is important for IT support and administration.

Practical Relevance

These are controls I could encounter when supporting Microsoft 365 users, particularly when troubleshooting sign-in problems, MFA issues, account lockouts or access policies.

Next Step

Practise these concepts in my Microsoft 365 / Entra environment, particularly MFA, Conditional Access and reviewing sign-in logs, to move from understanding the concepts to applying them.
